WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.335
File system analysis helps analysts understand when files were created, modified, accessed, moved, or deleted.

00:00:07.335 --> 00:00:13.336
NTFS, at a high level, stores metadata that can support timeline reconstruction.

00:00:13.336 --> 00:00:17.115
Timestamps are useful, but they require caution.

00:00:17.115 --> 00:00:25.784
Different actions update different timestamps, and tools, synchronization software, system processes, and investigator activity can all affect them.

00:00:25.784 --> 00:00:30.452
Execution traces can provide clues about programs that ran.

00:00:30.452 --> 00:00:35.564
Prefetch, when enabled, is commonly associated with program execution traces.

00:00:35.564 --> 00:00:46.455
Amcache and Shimcache, at a high level, may provide information related to program presence or execution-related activity depending on the system and interpretation.

00:00:46.455 --> 00:00:54.235
These artifacts can be valuable, but they should not be treated as perfect proof in isolation.

00:00:54.235 --> 00:01:02.904
Link files, jump lists, recent file artifacts, and recycle bin records can help reconstruct user and file interaction.

00:01:02.904 --> 00:01:11.572
They may show file paths, access patterns, timestamps, volume information, or references to removable media and network locations.

00:01:11.572 --> 00:01:20.685
Deleted file considerations depend on collection method, disk activity, system settings, and whether the evidence was preserved quickly enough.

00:01:20.685 --> 00:01:23.575
Timelines bring these pieces together.

00:01:23.575 --> 00:01:34.911
A useful Windows timeline may combine event logs, registry timestamps, file metadata, Prefetch, Amcache, Shimcache, link files, browser history, EDR telemetry, and application logs.

00:01:34.911 --> 00:01:42.246
The analyst should separate observed facts from interpretation, note time zone issues, and identify gaps.

00:01:42.246 --> 00:01:47.358
The goal is a clear sequence that can withstand review.
